    "stdout": "[{\"type\": \"web_search_result\", \"title\": \"A Translation of Mertens, R. 1956. Andrew M. Durso\", \"url\": \"https://repository.si.edu/bitstream/handle/10088/117310/2023.SHIS.160.MertensKorallenschlangen.pdf?sequence=1&amp=&isAllowed=y\", \"content\": \"while a magnificent Erythrolamprus aesculapii venustissimus is an exclusive snake eater and.\\n\\ncoral snake phenotype^**10**.\\n\\nThe coral snake phenotype is a form of mimicry.\\n\\nsnake phenotype in 4 subfamilies and among both the aglyphous and opisthoglyphous series.\\n\\nXenodontinae (opisthoglyphous series): *Erythrolamprus, Pseudoboa*.\\n\\ndepredating an *Erythrolamprus aesculapii* in central Brazil, and some coral snakes are also expected\\n\\nIn contrast, *Erythrolamprus aesculapii* seems to be a\\n\\nThe advantage of the coral snake\\n\\n(*Simophis, Erythrolamprus*) containing only snakes with a coral snake phenotype; however, there\\n\\na coral snake phenotype.\\n\\nrequirement applies to coral snake mimics.\\n\\n*Erythrolamprus aesculapii* (32) and *Simophis rhinostoma* (70).\\n\\nopisthoglyphous snake, also shows little tendency to bite when caught.\\n\\nthe large, lively, opisthoglyphous snake *Erythrolamprus aesculapii venustissimus* is very\\n\\n(opisthoglyphous) dentition such as *Erythrolamprus aesculapii* and *Pseudoboa trigemina* in South\\n\\n*Erythrolamprus,* which includes both opisthoglyphous and aglyphous forms This ushered in the\\n\\nsecond stage in the development of coral snake mimicry, which stands by and large before us today.\\n\\nThi\n",
